SUMMARY:Mapping the Election: The Impact of Historic and Modern Maps on the Midterms NEW
DESCRIPTION:This is a paid Adult School class. Click here to 
 register.\n\nAll eyes are on the midterm elections\, and maps--both old and 
 new--not only show how people vote but often help set and reinforce the 
 patterns of how citizens are divided. First we'll take a look at maps of 
 gerrymandering\, which started in Massachusetts in 1811 and have taken very 
 specific forms in the United States because of the rules of the American 
 political system. We'll move onto the familiar red and blue election maps 
 and examine how they privilege big\, sparsely populated states like Wyoming 
 over dense locations like New Jersey. We'll also take quick looks at great 
 historical images\, like Civil War-era election maps whose sectional splits 
 look shockingly modern.
LOCATION:Montclair Bellevue Ave Branch - Kids Place\, Montclair Public Library
ORGANIZER;CN="Montclair Adult School":MAILTO:adultschool@montclairlibrary.org
CATEGORIES:Adult School, Lectures / History & Culture
UID:LibCal-10306-17240140
URL:https://bccls.libcal.com/event/17240140
END:VEVENT
