X-SOURCE:Libcal Bccls 10315
DTSTART:20261103T190000Z
DTEND:20261103T203000Z
DTSTAMP:20261103T000000Z
SUMMARY:Library Speakers Consortium - Reclaiming Your Inner Wisdom: Sacred Indigenous Teachings with Asha Frost
DESCRIPTION:You must register here to attend:  
 https://libraryc.org/nutleypubliclibrary/217344\n\n\n\nIn a fast-paced 
 world that often leaves us feeling overwhelmed and disconnected\, true 
 healing does not come from looking outward—it begins within. Join us for 
 a transformative virtual conversation with Indigenous Medicine Woman Asha 
 Frost as she explores her groundbreaking book\, You Are the Medicine: 13 
 Indigenous Teachings to Connect to Your Inner Wisdom\, Reclaim Your Power\, 
 and Heal.\n\nThe Medicine you have been searching for lives within you. 
 Follow the path of the 13 Ojibwe Moons with Animal Spirits and Ancestors as 
 your guides as you unlock your connection to your own unique\, inherent 
 healing power. Through storytelling\, ceremonies\, and Shamanic journeys\, 
 learn to apply ancient wisdom to your life in ways that are respectful and 
 conscious of the stolen lands\, lives\, and traditions of Indigenous 
 peoples.\n\nDiscover how to:\n\n- Ground and root into your own lineage and 
 meet your Ancestral guides.\n- Practice self-care and rest on your 
 journey.\n- Return to Ancestral ways of cleansing and purifying.\n- Trust 
 and surrender so you can manifest and thrive.\n- Release self-doubt\, 
 fear\, disconnection\, and insecurity.\n\nRegister now and take the first 
 step toward recognizing and reclaiming your innate wisdom. \n\nAbout the 
 Author: \n\nAsha Frost (she/her) is an Indigenous Medicine Woman and a 
 member of the Chippewas of Nawash First Nation. She is from the Crane 
 Clan\, the totem of leadership\, and believes in holding space from vision 
 and heart. As an energy healer\, homeopath\, and mentor\, Asha has guided 
 thousands of people through profound and lasting 
 transformation.\n\nImpacted by generational trauma and colonization\, Asha 
 has been on a lifelong journey of reclamation. A lupus diagnosis sent her 
 on a path of studying and practicing a multitude of energy Medicine 
 modalities with many guides. She has blended this life experience with her 
 innate gifts and the wisdom of her Ancestors. She loves sharing her 
 Medicine in powerful ways through Ceremonies\, teachings\, and speaking 
 events. Through this work\, she loves seeing people reclaim their roots\, 
 find their healing wisdom\, and rise into their power.\n\nAsha lives on 
 Anishinaabe\, Huron-Wendat\, and Haudenosaunee Territory\, with her husband 
 and two beautiful children\, with whom she co-creates a better world for 
 the seven generations to come.\n\n\n\n
LOCATION:Nutley Free Public Library
ORGANIZER;CN="Nutley Library":MAILTO:events@nutley.bccls.org
CATEGORIES:Author Event/Talk
UID:LibCal-10315-17621803
URL:https://bccls.libcal.com/event/17621803
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Campuslabs Montclair": "https://montclair.campuslabs.com/engage/event/12650840"}
X-SOURCE-ID:campuslabs_montclair
X-SOURCE:Montclair State University
CATEGORIES:ThoughtfulLearning
CATEGORIES:Civics
DESCRIPTION:\n\n\n\nJoin President Koppell for his first lecture in the Am
 erican Experiment series\, where he will discuss Information\, Power\, an
 d Democracy: The First Amendment.\nThe First Amendment is the cornerstone
  of American democracy because it guarantees the fundamental freedoms req
 uired for citizens to govern themselves\, including freedom of speech\, f
 reedom of the press\, and freedom of assembly.  Citizens need access to o
 pen debate\, diverse viewpoints\, and accurate information in order to ma
 ke informed decisions. These freedoms also protect unpopular and minority
  opinions and act as a check on the state from censoring speech or contro
 lling what people learn\, think\, and say.  This lecture examines the fou
 ndational freedoms of the First Amendment and how they are applied to con
 temporary American democratic life\, including: the impact of money on ex
 pression\, the role of new media and corporate media\, the role of the pr
 ess in investigating and disseminating information\, the right to protest
  as a form of assembly and expression\, and the challenges posed by misin
 formation\, disinformation\, polarization\, fragmented media audiences\, 
 and declining public trust. At its center is a fundamental question for A
 merican democracy: What are the inherent rights and responsibilities of f
 reedom of expression in a democratic society?\n\n\n\n\n\nHosted by: Campu
 s Activities\, Civic & Voter Engagement\n\nAdditional Information can be 
 found at: https://montclair.campuslabs.com/engage/event/12650840
DTEND:20261103T210000Z
DTSTAMP:20261010T052731Z
DTSTART:20261103T193000Z
LOCATION:Presentation Hall
SEQUENCE:0
STATUS:CONFIRMED
SUMMARY:The American Experiment: Information\, Power\, and Democracy: The 
 First Amendment
UID:https://montclair.campuslabs.com/engage/event/12650840
URL:https://montclair.campuslabs.com/engage/event/12650840
X-HOSTS:Civic & Voter Engagement
END:VEVENT
